From: lckt0 <lckt0@protonmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] compositor: repaint an output layer when it gets a new item

When KWin's overlay-plane support is enabled (KWIN_USE_OVERLAYS=1, needed on
amdgpu), Compositor::setupLayers() can move the scene (containerItem) to a
different OutputLayer, for example when a notification or tray popup that had
its own overlay plane closes. It only calls sceneView->setLayer(layer), and
RenderView::setLayer() does not schedule a repaint. renderLayers() renders only
layers that need a repaint, so the newly assigned layer is not rendered that
frame and is presented with whatever buffer it held before (the closing popup),
while the scene's previous layer is turned off. Result: windows and panels
vanish for ~10 frames and only the wallpaper and the popup are visible.

The same can happen when a new ItemView is put on an overlay layer.

Schedule a full device repaint on the layer whenever its assignment changes.
The cost is one full repaint on the (rare) frames where layers are reassigned.

Tested on an ASUS ROG Zephyrus G14 (GA403GM, Radeon 880M / DCN 3.5) with
KWin 6.8.0. This copy is rebased onto KWin 6.8.1, which reorganised the cursor
timer code around the second insertion point; the change itself is identical.
Built against KWin 6.8.1 (Arch 6.8.1-2 sources).
---
--- a/src/compositor.cpp
+++ b/src/compositor.cpp
@@ -663,6 +663,12 @@
         const bool isCursor = qobject_cast<CursorItem *>(item) != nullptr;
         const bool isScene = item == kwinApp()->scene()->containerItem();
         if (isScene) {
+            if (sceneView->layer() != layer) {
+                // the layer still holds the buffer of whatever was assigned to it before
+                // (for example a closing popup). Without a full repaint it isn't rendered
+                // this frame and that stale buffer gets presented as the whole scene
+                layer->addDeviceRepaint(Region::infinite());
+            }
             sceneView->setLayer(layer);
             underlay = false;
             layers.push_back(LayerData{
@@ -733,6 +739,8 @@
                 view = std::make_unique<ItemView>(sceneView, item, logicalOutput, backendOutput, layer);
                 timers.erase(layer);
             }
+            // same as for the scene: the layer may still show the previous item's buffer
+            layer->addDeviceRepaint(Region::infinite());
         } else {
             const auto it = timers.find(layer);
             if (it != timers.end()) {
